__pycache__/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
export_presets.cfg

# Godot import metadata: regenerated per machine, and a guaranteed pull conflict when the
# project is opened on both a PC and an iPad. The screenshots in docs/ and the art in
# art/references/ are data, not textures, so each carries a .gdignore and Godot never imports
# them.
#
# terrain/ USED TO, and must not. A .gdignore does not only hide a folder from the editor: the
# exporter cannot see it either, and no export filter reaches back in - "*.r16" in the preset
# looked right and shipped nothing. The build ran, failed to open res://terrain/island.r16,
# and came up with no island at all. Godot does not import .r16 anyway, so the folder costs
# nothing being visible; the two .png height maps beside them are imported now, which is the
# whole price.
*.import

# ...except the few that carry settings which exist NOWHERE ELSE. A rigged model's scale has
# to be nodes/root_scale, because scaling above a Skeleton3D breaks skinning - so ignoring
# these meant a fresh clone regenerated them with defaults and got a captain at 53% height
# with a broken jump. tests/import_check.gd fails if any of them is ever lost again.
#
# An UNRIGGED model does not need an exception: tools/reorient_model.py --scale bakes the size
# into the .glb, which is why fish_blue.glb has root_scale 1.0 and is not listed here. The arch
# is listed rather than baked only because it is already placed by hand in main.tscn and a
# re-bake would be a chance to move it.
!art/models/captain.glb.import
!art/models/grunt.glb.import
!art/models/shark.glb.import
!art/models/rocks/rock_arch.glb.import

# Generated by ship_kit/tools/build_kit.py - 18 modules and 4 assemblies, rebuilt in seconds.
ship_kit/canonical/
